### Description

Stack overflow exception is thrown by `JsonSerializer.Deserialize` for a special case object when .NET 8 uses code generated by previous .NET versions.

### Reproduction Steps

a .NET 8 application
```cs
using System.Text.Json;

using Lib;

JsonSerializer.Deserialize("{}", X.XSerializerContext.Default.X);
```
a .NET 6/7 (I only tested with these versions) library referenced by the application
```cs
using System.Text.Json.Serialization;

namespace Lib;

public class XBase
{
}

public partial class X : XBase
{
    public Y? Z { get; set; }

    [JsonSerializable(typeof(X))]
    public partial class XSerializerContext : JsonSerializerContext
    {
    }
}

public class Y
{
    public X? Q { get; set; }
}
```

### Expected behavior

Stack overflow doesn't occur.

### Actual behavior

Stack overflow occurs.

### Regression?

_No response_

### Known Workarounds

_No response_

### Configuration

.NET 8: 8.0.100-preview.3.23178.7

### Other information

_No response_

comments:
I can reproduce, but it only occurs if you're using a `net6.0` class library on a `net8.0` application. Minimal reproduction:

## `net6.0` Class Library

```C#
public class MyPoco
{
    public MyPoco? Nested { get; set; }
}

[JsonSerializable(typeof(MyPoco))]
public partial class MyContext : JsonSerializerContext { }
```

## `net8.0` Console App

```C#
_ = MyContext.Default.MyPoco;
```

This has been regressed by https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/81306, which removed delayed initialization for properties. This appears to be breaking the .NET 6 generator, where the generated factory for `JsonTypeInfo<MyPoco>` directly references itself making delayed initialization essential:
```C#
public global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonTypeInfo<global::MyPoco> MyPoco
{
    get
    {
        if (_MyPoco == null)
        {
            global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonObjectInfoValues<global::MyPoco> objectInfo = new global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonObjectInfoValues<global::MyPoco>()
            {
                PropertyMetadataInitializer = MyPocoPropInit,
            };

            _MyPoco = global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonMetadataServices.CreateObjectInfo<global::MyPoco>(Options, objectInfo);
        }

        return _MyPoco;
    }
}

private static global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonPropertyInfo[] MyPocoPropInit(global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.JsonSerializerContext context)
{
    global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonPropertyInfo[] properties = new global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonPropertyInfo[1];

    global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonPropertyInfoValues<global::MyPoco> info0 = new global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonPropertyInfoValues<global::MyPoco>()
    {
        PropertyTypeInfo = jsonContext.MyPoco,
    };

    properties[0] = global::System.Text.Json.Serialization.Metadata.JsonMetadataServices.CreatePropertyInfo<global::MyPoco>(options, info0);

    return properties;
}
```

We should try to fix this, possibly by adding some form of runtime detection of `JsonSerializerContext` types generated by the `.NET 6` SDK and preventing SO conditions.
